    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d447">

                <pb facs="0917" n="831" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg831"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Hopkins Papers</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to the President’s Special
                        Assistant</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d447-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Casablanca</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-01-21">January 21, 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Hopkins</hi>:</p>
                <p>Can I take it that the provisional programme, on the assumption that the
                        bride<anchor n="2" xml:id="d447-f2"/> arrives tomorrow morning, is as
                    follows:— <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Friday, 22nd</hi>.</p>
                        <p>Interviews with Giraud and de Gaulle, and their talks among themselves
                            begin.</p>
                        <pb facs="0918" n="832" xml:id="pg832"/>
                        <p>Luncheon.</p>
                        <p>Would it not be well to have both French chiefs to lunch either with
                            Admiral Q or here?</p>
                        <p>Afternoon at 5.0.</p>
                        <p>Report of the Chiefs of the Staff on the quantitative aspects of their
                            recent conversations on the Paper they wrote the other day.<anchor n="3"
                                xml:id="d447-f3"/></p>
                        <p>Dinner. At the White House. (Dry, alas!); with the Sultan. After dinner,
                            recovery from the effects of the above.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d447-f4"
                            /></p>
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Saturday, 23rd</hi>.</p>
                        <p>9.0 a.m. Press Conference.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d447-f5"/></p>
                        <p>10.0 a.m. Photographs.<anchor n="6" xml:id="d447-f6"/></p>
                        <p>Thereafter Admiral Q and I leave for airfield but really go to M.,<anchor
                                n="7" xml:id="d447-f7"/> where we arrive (pretty hungry) for lunch.
                            The British Chiefs of the Staff are all coming to M.<anchor n="8"
                                xml:id="d447-f8"/></p>
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Sunday, 24th</hi>.</p>
                        <p>Polish off the wedding party and tidy up anything else left.</p>
                        <p><hi rendition="#italic">Monday, 25th</hi>.</p>
                        <p>Admiral Q departs. Where would he like to meet Lord Swinton,<anchor n="9"
                                xml:id="d447-f9"/> Accra, Bathurst, Freetown? I can arrange anything
                            but I should like them to meet.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Will you kindly check the above and amplify it in any way so that I can tell my
                    people.</p>
                <p>21.1.43.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d447-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d447-f1-content">
                <p>This message, which is on the Prime Minister’s 10 Downing Street letterhead,
                    bears no signature but is obviously from Churchill.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f2" n="2" xml:id="d447-f2-content">
                <p>Reference here is to de Gaulle who did arrive at Casablanca on January 22. See
                    Roosevelt’s reference to de Gaulle in the January 18 telegram to Hull, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg816">p. 816</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f3" n="3" xml:id="d447-f3-content">
                <p>The Final Report to the President and Prime Minister by the Combined Chiefs of
                    Staff, the final text of which was designated C.C.S. 170/2, January 23, 1943, is
                    printed <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg791">p. 791</ref>.
                    For the meeting of the Combined Chiefs of Staff with Roosevelt and Churchill on
                    January 23, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg707">p.
                        707</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f4" n="4" xml:id="d447-f4-content">
                <p>Regarding Roosevelt’s January 22 dinner party for the Sultan, see the editorial
                    note, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg693">p. 693</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f5" n="5" xml:id="d447-f5-content">
                <p>The press conference was, in fact, scheduled for noon of January 22, but it was
                    postponed because of the failure to reach agreement on the statement to be
                    issued and in order to await the outcome of the meeting between de Gaulle and
                    Giraud. Regarding the postponement, see Hopkins’ account in Sherwood, p. 687.
                    For the record of the press conference, eventually held on the morning of
                    January 24, see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg726">p.
                        726</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f6" n="6" xml:id="d447-f6-content">
                <p>Photographs! follow <ref target="#pg483">p. 483</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f7" n="7" xml:id="d447-f7-content">
                <p>i.e., Marrakech. The President and the Prime Minister did not travel to Marrakech
                    until January 24; see the Log, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg534">p. 534</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f8" n="8" xml:id="d447-f8-content">
                <p>Only General Brooke made the trip to Marrakech; see Alanbrooke, p. 459.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d447-f9" n="9" xml:id="d447-f9-content">
                <p>In the course of his return trip to the United States, the President conferred
                    briefly with Lord Swinton aboard the cruiser U.S.S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Memphis</hi> in the harbor at Bathurst.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
